`internal/cli/publish.go:477–549`: artifact collection neither checks the tombstone policy nor filters fact provenance, and runPublish has no privacy lock covering collection/send. During a failed or in-progress cleanup, a single-source excluded fact is included in the upload payload. TestAuditPublishReadsExcludedFact confirms the payload; no external upload was performed. Apply the current privacy policy and the established side-effect serialization to publishing. Evidence: audit-publishnew*, audit-publishfacts*.

## F62 — P1: Publish follows parent-directory symlinks outside the brain
`internal/cli/publish.go:477–506, 736–754`: collection follows a symlinked facts root; readBrainBlob checks only the leaf. TestAuditPublishReadsOutsideSymlinkRoot confirms external fact bytes enter the publish artifact. Similar parent exposure exists in snapshot/overlay collection. Use a brain-root-bound reader and reject symlinked parents before artifact enumeration/read. Evidence: audit-publishnew*, audit-publishfacts*.

## F63 — P2: Publish silently omits a malformed first fact record
`internal/cli/publish.go:513–516, 766–781`: firstFactBranch returns an empty branch on parse failure, so the stream is skipped before redactFactsForEgress can reject it. A malformed later record errors, but a malformed first record silently excludes the whole store from a successful publish. TestAuditPublishSkipsMalformedFirstFact confirms this. Parse/validate the complete stream before deciding its branch; surface malformed/ambiguous stores. Evidence: audit-publishnew*.

## F64 — P2: Publish treats an unrelated partial acknowledgement as success
`internal/cli/publish.go:650–668`: acknowledgement validation only checks that stored is nonempty. A response naming one unrelated coordinate succeeds for an upload containing two different artifacts. TestAuditPublishPartialAckAndToken/true confirms it. Compare the acknowledged coordinate set against the submitted set and report omissions/duplicates. Evidence: audit-publishnew*, audit-publishack*.

## F65 — P2: Publish's HTTP-200 error path leaks an echoed bearer token
`internal/cli/publish.go:664–665, 692–699`: publishStatusDetail cleans terminal controls but does not redact the request token, unlike publishServerDetail. A 200 with empty stored and status equal to the fake token includes that token in the returned error. TestAuditPublishPartialAckAndToken/false confirms it with a synthetic token and loopback server. Redact credentials consistently on all response-error paths. Evidence: audit-publishnew*, audit-publishack*.


